          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "glog-e5caba8c-a460-430b-8a47-e32b11fb8fb7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `shell=True` argument in the `subprocess.run()` function in Python is a security vulnerability. It allows for shell injection, a form of attack that can be used to execute arbitrary commands. This is especially dangerous if your program is running with special privileges.\n\nThe `subprocess.run()` function is used to spawn a new process and run the specified command. When `shell=True` is used, the specified command is executed through the shell. This can be exploited if an attacker is able to control the command string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `shell=True` whenever possible. Instead, pass the command and its arguments as a list. This way, Python will take care of any necessary escaping.\n\nIf you must use `shell=True`, be sure to properly escape any user-supplied input to prevent shell injection attacks. The `shlex.quote()` function can be used to safely escape any string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can modify the code to avoid this vulnerability:\n\n```python\nimport subprocess\nimport shlex\n\n# Assume commandstring is a user-supplied string\nsafe_command = shlex.quote(commandstring)\nproc = subprocess.run(safe_command, shell=True, check=True, executable='/bin/bash')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `subprocess` and `shlex` modules, which are both part of the Python Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Shell Injection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B602"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-595b5bcf-152f-4575-81c3-498a9af318e0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `shell=True` argument in the `subprocess.run()` function in Python is a security vulnerability. It allows for shell injection, a form of attack that can execute arbitrary commands on the host's shell. This is especially dangerous if the command string is formed using external input. An attacker can potentially execute harmful commands, leading to data loss, data theft, or server takeover.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `shell=True` whenever possible, especially if the command string includes external input. If you need to use shell features, consider using the `shlex` library to safely create command strings. Always validate and sanitize input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```python\nproc = subprocess.run(commandstring, shell=True, check=True, executable='/bin/bash', stdout=subprocess.PIPE)\n```\n\nYou can use:\n\n```python\nimport shlex\ncommand_list = shlex.split(commandstring)\nproc = subprocess.run(command_list, check=True, executable='/bin/bash', stdout=subprocess.PIPE)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `subprocess` library, which is included in the Python Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B602"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-c4149b1b-09af-402e-9168-4a717c8fd8ad",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Vulnerability in URL Audit for Permitted Schemes\" in Python refers to a situation where an application does not properly validate the schemes of the URLs it processes. This can lead to a situation where an attacker can manipulate the URL to access unauthorized resources or perform malicious activities. In the provided code, the `urlopen` function is used to open a URL specified by the user. If the URL scheme is not validated, it can lead to various security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate the scheme of the URL before processing it. Only allow URLs with safe schemes such as `http` or `https`. Avoid using `file`, `data`, or any other scheme that can potentially access local resources. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```python\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse\nimport urllib.request\n\ndef open_url(request):\n    url = urlparse(request)\n    if url.scheme not in ['http', 'https']:\n        raise ValueError('Invalid URL scheme')\n    return urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=300)\n```\n\nIn this code, the `urlparse` function is used to parse the URL and extract the scheme. If the scheme is not `http` or `https`, a `ValueError` is raised.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python standard library modules:\n\n- `urllib.parse`\n- `urllib.request`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-918: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html)\n\nPlease note that the CWE link provided is for Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) which is a common attack that exploits the vulnerability in URL audit for permitted schemes."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B310"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-1f171e8c-0db2-4fec-829c-dfeed96f7bbd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unvalidated tarfile.extractall usage vulnerability\" in Python refers to a security flaw that can occur when a Python script uses the `tarfile.extractall()` method without validating the contents of the tarfile. This can lead to a path traversal attack, where an attacker can create a tarfile that, when extracted, overwrites arbitrary files on the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate the paths of the files in the tarfile before extraction. This can be done by checking each file's path in the tarfile and ensuring that it does not contain directory traversal sequences (like \"../\"). \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a sample code fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport tarfile\n\nwith tarfile.open('sample.tar', 'r') as tar:\n    for member in tar.getmembers():\n        # Check for directory traversal attack\n        if os.path.isabs(member.name) or \"..\" in member.name:\n            print(f\"Skipping potentially unsafe tarfile member {member.name}\")\n            continue\n        tar.extract(member, path=package_root_dir)\n```\n\nIn this code, we iterate over each member of the tarfile and check if its name contains an absolute path or a directory traversal sequence. If it does, we skip that member and do not extract it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` and `tarfile` libraries, which are both part of the Python Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B202"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-4612e9ae-b89d-42c1-96cb-b78e97e4992f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `shell=True` argument in the `subprocess.run()` function in Python is a security vulnerability. It allows for shell injection, a form of attack that can be used to execute arbitrary commands. This is especially dangerous if your program is running with special privileges.\n\nThe `subprocess.run()` function is used to spawn a new process and run the specified command. When `shell=True` is used, the specified command is executed through the shell. This can be exploited if an attacker is able to control the command string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `shell=True` whenever possible. Instead, pass the command and its arguments as a list. This way, Python will take care of any necessary escaping.\n\nIf you must use `shell=True`, be sure to properly escape any user-supplied input to prevent shell injection attacks. The `shlex.quote()` function can be used to safely escape any string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can modify the code to avoid this vulnerability:\n\n```python\nimport subprocess\nimport shlex\n\n# Assume commandstring is a user-supplied string\nsafe_command = shlex.quote(commandstring)\nproc = subprocess.run(safe_command, shell=True, check=True, executable='/bin/bash')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `subprocess` and `shlex` modules, which are both part of the Python Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Shell Injection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B602"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-ba07a820-ca70-445e-a074-31e54d191af3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `shell=True` argument in the `subprocess.run()` function in Python is a security vulnerability. It allows for shell injection, a form of attack that can execute arbitrary commands on the host's shell. This is especially dangerous if the command string is formed using external input. An attacker can potentially execute harmful commands, leading to data loss, data theft, or server takeover.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `shell=True` whenever possible, especially if the command string includes external input. If you need to use shell features, consider using the `shlex` library to safely create command strings. Always validate and sanitize input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```python\nproc = subprocess.run(commandstring, shell=True, check=True, executable='/bin/bash', stdout=subprocess.PIPE)\n```\n\nYou can use:\n\n```python\nimport shlex\ncommand_list = shlex.split(commandstring)\nproc = subprocess.run(command_list, check=True, executable='/bin/bash', stdout=subprocess.PIPE)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `subprocess` library, which is included in the Python Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
