            {
              "id": "glog-48839056-3fe1-4ad3-a5d1-ac6024fc20b9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Exec Usage Detected\" is a vulnerability in Python programming language that arises when the `exec()` function is used. The `exec()` function is a built-in Python function that is used to execute dynamically created program, which can be a string or object code. If this function is used, it can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code, leading to serious security risks such as code injection, data leakage, and even server takeover.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `exec()` function whenever possible. If it's absolutely necessary to use it, make sure that the input passed to `exec()` is not coming directly from user input or any untrusted source. Always validate and sanitize the input before passing it to `exec()`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `exec()`, consider using safer alternatives such as `getattr()`, `setattr()`, or `hasattr()`. If you must use `exec()`, ensure that the input is safe. Here's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```python\n# BAD CODE\nexec(compiled_code, fn_globals, local_namespace)\n\n# GOOD CODE\n# Ensure that compiled_code is safe before executing it\nif is_code_safe(compiled_code):\n    exec(compiled_code, fn_globals, local_namespace)\n```\n\nIn the above code, `is_code_safe()` is a hypothetical function that you would need to implement. This function should thoroughly check `compiled_code` to ensure that it doesn't contain any malicious code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `exec()` function is a built-in Python function, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B102"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-9f51037e-bef3-427c-8f29-05b1f262fbb8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Exec Usage Detected\" is a vulnerability in Python programming language that arises when the `exec()` function is used. The `exec()` function is a powerful built-in function in Python that dynamically executes Python program which can either be a string or object code. If not properly sanitized, it can be exploited by an attacker to execute arbitrary commands leading to serious security issues such as code injection, data leakage, and even remote code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `exec()` function whenever possible. If it's absolutely necessary to use it, ensure that the input passed to `exec()` is properly sanitized and validated to prevent any malicious code from being executed. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `exec()`, consider using safer alternatives such as `getattr()`, `setattr()`, or `locals()`. If you must use `exec()`, ensure to sanitize and validate the input. Here is an example:\n\n```python\n# BAD\nexec(func_body, func_namespace)\n\n# GOOD\n# Validate and sanitize func_body and func_namespace before using them\nif isinstance(func_body, str) and isinstance(func_namespace, dict):\n    sanitized_func_body = sanitize_input(func_body)\n    sanitized_func_namespace = sanitize_input(func_namespace)\n    exec(sanitized_func_body, sanitized_func_namespace)\n```\n\nIn the above code, `sanitize_input()` is a hypothetical function that you would implement to sanitize and validate the input.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `exec()` function is a built-in function in Python and does not require any additional library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
