            {
              "id": "glog-4e9cf7e6-6051-41e7-a94a-ee6104f6960f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `tarfile.extractall` function in Python is used to extract all members of the archive to the current working directory or to the directory specified. However, if the usage of this function is not properly validated, it can lead to a vulnerability known as path traversal. This vulnerability can allow an attacker to write files to arbitrary locations through a \"..\" (dot dot) path, potentially leading to code execution, privilege escalation, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate the paths of the files being extracted from the tar archive. This can be done by checking if the file path is absolute or if it contains \"..\" (dot dot), which can be used to traverse directories. If either of these conditions is true, the file should not be extracted.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to validate the paths of the files being extracted:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport tarfile\n\nwith tarfile.open('archive.tar.gz', 'r:gz') as tar:\n    for member in tar.getmembers():\n        # Check if file path is absolute or contains \"..\"\n        if os.path.isabs(member.name) or \"..\" in member.name:\n            print(f\"Skipping potentially dangerous file {member.name}\")\n            continue\n        tar.extract(member, path=tmp_path)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python standard library:\n\n- `os`\n- `tarfile`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B202"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-3b9069d2-93d2-4b95-b899-0bbdf8baa0fb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unvalidated tarfile.extractall usage vulnerability\" in Python refers to a security flaw that can occur when a Python script uses the `tarfile.extractall()` method without validating the contents of the tarfile. This can lead to a path traversal attack, where an attacker can create a tarfile that, when extracted, overwrites arbitrary files on the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate the paths of the files in the tarfile before extraction. This can be done by checking each file's path in the tarfile and ensuring that it does not contain directory traversal sequences (like \"../\"). \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a sample code fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport tarfile\n\nwith tarfile.open('sample.tar', 'r') as tar:\n    for member in tar.getmembers():\n        # Check for directory traversal attack\n        if os.path.isabs(member.name) or \"..\" in member.name:\n            print(f\"Skipping potentially unsafe tarfile member {member.name}\")\n            continue\n        tar.extract(member, path=package_root_dir)\n```\n\nIn this code, we iterate over each member of the tarfile and check if its name contains an absolute path or a directory traversal sequence. If it does, we skip that member and do not extract it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` and `tarfile` libraries, which are both part of the Python Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
